ms-a2-fancontrol (0.5.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  * CPU fan curve: the CPU fan also follows the CPU temperature (Tctl),
    linear from CPU_LOW 80 °C to CPU_HIGH 90 °C. It runs at the higher of
    the RAM and the CPU value. Before, the CPU temperature only triggered
    the emergency level, which caused a sawtooth on a Ryzen 9 7945HX:
    full speed at 92 °C, slow fall back to level 16, again 92 °C a few
    minutes later (52 emergencies in 2.5 hours under nested PVE load).
  * CPU_LEVEL_MAX raised from 40 to 50 (also the top of the RAM curve).
  * CPU_EMERGENCY raised from 92 to 95 °C.
  * Emergency hysteresis: the emergency level stays until every sensor
    is EMERGENCY_HYST (5) °C below its threshold.
  * "status" shows what set the CPU fan level (ram, cpu, emergency or
    firmware) and the number of emergencies since the service started,
    from the state file /run/ms-a2-fancontrol.state. The journal logs
    the start and end of each emergency.

 -- ProxForge <ms-a2-fancontrol@proxforge.invalid>  Thu, 12 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0100

ms-a2-fancontrol (0.4.0) trixie; urgency=medium

  * New command "dump": read-only report of DMI data, CPU model, sensors,
    EC RAM 0x00-0xff, fan curve values and fan speeds. Runs on any MS-A2
    BIOS version and never writes to the EC, for adding support for
    other firmware.
  * Unknown device, BIOS version or EC values: exit status 3 with a short
    message instead of a traceback; the unit no longer restarts the
    service every 10 seconds (RestartPreventExitStatus=3).
  * README: only two onboard M.2 NVMe drives tested; notes on other drive
    setups (U.2/U.3, PCIe cards).

 -- ProxForge <ms-a2-fancontrol@proxforge.invalid>  Wed, 11 Nov 2026 22:00:00 +0100

